<LessonShell chapterNumber={3} lessonNumber={3}>

## The Type You Met First

You have been using strings since your very first `print()`. Lesson 3.2 gave you the vocabulary for what they are: text is a type, called `str`, and it sits alongside `int` and `float` as one of the kinds of value Python deals in.

This lesson is about what makes `str` different from the other two — and it is one thing, from which everything else follows.

> Chapter 5 is a whole chapter on strings: formatting, escape characters, and the toolkit of methods that do the real work. This lesson is not that. This lesson is strings **as a type**, because that is what you need before Lesson 3.4 can compare them and Chapter 6 can generalise them.

## A String Has Parts

The number `4` is one thing. There is no "inside" of it, nothing to count, no first half.

The string `"Karel"` is not like that. It is **five characters in a row**, and that changes what you can ask about it.

The first question is how many, and `len()` answers it:

<PythonEnvironment config={lengthConfig} />

Three things worth noticing in that output:

- `len("a b c")` is `5`, not `3`. Spaces are characters. They take up room and they count.
- `len("")` is `0`. The empty string is a perfectly ordinary string that happens to contain nothing — you will meet it again in Lesson 3.5, where it is what `input()` hands back when somebody just presses Enter.
- `len()` works on the value, not the name. `len(word)` and `len("Karel")` are the same question.

## Reaching In: Index Positions

Because a string has parts, you can ask for one of them. Square brackets do that, and the number inside is the **index** — the position you want:

```python
word = "Karel"
print(word[0])
```

That prints `K`, and if you expected `a`, you have just met the single most important convention in programming: **counting starts at nought.**

```
 K   a   r   e   l
 0   1   2   3   4
```

The first character is at index `0`, and the last is at index `4` — one less than the length. This is not Python being awkward; nearly every language does it, and Chapter 6 will show you why it makes list arithmetic come out clean.

<PythonEnvironment config={indexConfig} />

**Negative indexes count from the other end.** `word[-1]` is the last character, `word[-2]` the one before it. This is a genuine convenience: `word[-1]` says "the last one" directly, where the alternative is to work it out.

```python
word[len(word) - 1]   # the last character, the long way
word[-1]              # the last character
```

Both are correct and you should be able to read both. The long way matters because it is the version that survives when you meet a structure that has no negative indexing, and because working out `len(word) - 1` for yourself is what makes the off-by-one arithmetic stick.

### Asking for a Character That Isn't There

`"Karel"` has five characters, at positions `0` to `4`. So what does `word[5]` do?

<PythonEnvironment config={indexErrorConfig} />

`IndexError: string index out of range`. The program stops.

Get used to reading that message, because it is one you will cause many times, and it nearly always means the same thing: you used the **length** where you needed the **last index**. The length is `5`; the last index is `4`. Off by one, every time.

## Strings Cannot Be Changed

Here is the fact that catches people, and it is a type fact, which is why it belongs in this chapter rather than in Chapter 5.

<PythonEnvironment config={immutableConfig} />

`TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment`. You may **look at** any character of a string. You may not **replace** one.

Strings are **immutable** — a word meaning "cannot be changed after it is made". Every operation that looks like it changes a string in fact builds a brand new one:

```python
word = "karel"
word = "K" + word[1] + word[2] + word[3] + word[4]
```

That second line does not edit anything. It works out a whole new five-character string on the right, and then — exactly as Lesson 3.1 described — moves the label `word` onto it. The original `"karel"` is untouched; it simply has nothing pointing at it any more.

Lesson 3.1 promised that "reassigning a name affects that name and nothing else" would stop being the whole story in Chapter 6. Strings are not the exception. They are the reason the rule has held so far: when a value cannot be changed from underneath a label, the only way to change what a name means is to move the label, and moving one label never disturbs another. Chapter 6 introduces a type that **can** be changed in place, and that is where the second half of the story arrives.

## `+` and `*`, Explained at Last

Chapter 2 taught you two operations on strings and asked you to accept them as facts. Now they have a reason.

- **`+` joins two strings** into a new one. `"beep" + "er"` is `"beeper"`.
- **`*` repeats a string.** `"=" * 24` is a line of twenty-four equals signs, which is how you drew a banner in Lesson 2.2.

Chapter 2 also warned you that `"5" + 5` fails. Now you can say exactly why: `+` between two `str` values means join, and between two `int` values means add, and Python will not guess which one you meant when you hand it one of each. It refuses, with `TypeError`, rather than inventing an answer.

That an operator can mean different things to different types is a real Python feature with a real name — operator overloading — and Chapter 9 shows you how to give your own types the same trick.

