## Exercise 1: Measuring Up

Take a word apart at both ends.

Ask for a word, then report the word itself, how many letters it has, its first letter, and its last letter — the last one printed **twice**, once worked out the long way and once with a negative index.

A sample run, where the person typed **beeper**:

```
Give me a word:
The word: beeper
Letters: 6
First letter: b
Last letter: r
Also the last letter: r
```

Both ways of naming the last letter have to appear in your code. They print the same character, which is the point: you are checking your own arithmetic against Python's shorthand.

<PythonEnvironment config={exercise1Config} />

---

## Exercise 2: The Acronym

Build a short name out of long ones.

Ask for three words, then take the first letter of each, join them into an acronym, and report what you started with and what you ended up with.

A sample run, where the person typed **portable**, **network** and **graphics**:

```
First word:
Second word:
Third word:
Words: portable network graphics
Acronym: png
Kept 3 letters out of 23
```

The last line compares the two: three letters kept, out of the three words' lengths added together.

Build the acronym with `+` between three indexed characters, and store it in its own variable — you need its length on that last line.

<PythonEnvironment config={exercise2Config} />

